  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Tampa Bay was built to survive one-run games. The Yankees built to win them. They couldn't finish this one.",
    "what_this_means": "Ben Rice kept them alive with two home runs. The only real moments the offense created all night. But moments aren't enough when you never lead. They trailed from the first inning, chased all game, and when inning five broke open and Tampa took a 5-2 lead, the math stopped working. Cam Schlittler left after 4.1 innings having allowed just two runs, but the bullpen that followed. Headrick, Schreiber, Weathers, Blackburn, Bednar combining for 2.5 scoreless. Couldn't matter because the bats went quiet after Rice's solo shots. Four hits. Seven strikeouts. The lineup was shut down before it ever started. This is a team built on expectation, playing like they're trying to survive. Trailing teams don't have the luxury of waiting for the right moment. They need every at-bat to count. The Yankees got four at-bats that mattered and two of them left the yard. The other two left nothing.",
    "what_to_watch": "Nick Martinez takes the ball tomorrow night with a 3.10 ERA over his last handful. Max Fried is sharper. 2.93 over four starts. And the Yankees need a starter who commands the zone when the lineup is still finding itself.",
